the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420327 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420330 1069 "q-181641" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420330 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{1-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4{e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420330 1072 #uuid "a6b24cf2-2976-579e-8d0e-dd268f3c65a7" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420330 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{1-2x})=-2{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{1-2x})=4{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{1-2x})=-8{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{1-2x},-2{e}^{1-2x},4{e}^{1-2x},-8{e}^{1-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nB={3{e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{e}^{-x})=4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nC={4{e}^{-x},-4{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=2,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420325 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420331 1069 "q-181667" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420331 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420331 1072 #uuid "c8147626-87d7-56ca-be09-d1e1490d794e" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420331 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420326 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420332 1069 "q-181706" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420332 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420332 1072 #uuid "d8b98aed-4075-5dac-9091-d486170b806a" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true] [17592186420332 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})=-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}},-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\sqrt{x},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" 13194139534370 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-099f3a38-4da4-5649-a22d-eba7684b0cfe" 17592186420305 "answer-1137ea51-0849-5c10-9f4b-2be034d8e893" 17592186420306 "answer-1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" 17592186420307 "answer-15ca3f93-b276-5422-a4f0-a2e4f89be083" 17592186420308 "answer-1cb5e67c-cb84-5412-9a3e-61b4099aeabd" 17592186420309 "answer-3c6dea98-f9b5-5483-ad34-13ce6f559a2d" 17592186420310 "answer-43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" 17592186420311 "answer-486b0493-eb45-55ed-82f5-c032db770e60" 17592186420312 "answer-65e1ba83-f0a5-5cc9-8f4a-3ea942a47e04" 17592186420313 "answer-671c41b3-d8d4-5559-b179-e68f0f12a414" 17592186420314 "answer-6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" 17592186420315 "answer-872b5930-b555-5b00-977e-8ecaeb8ec451" 17592186420316 "answer-a0223240-2eb5-5bc2-b389-218324c528fe" 17592186420317 "answer-a0d2d1d1-0842-520f-b8fa-9b61ab89e6fd" 17592186420318 "answer-c84a5364-9750-5067-9737-5e4daa30aa9e" 17592186420319 "answer-c9a4b8b0-70fb-5140-bd89-b7cb08f1ab9f" 17592186420320 "answer-d21b5f4e-d145-5b7f-b843-065f3a5904e0" 17592186420321 "answer-dde382cc-266f-5db0-88f1-85b4627ded80" 1759